In C programming, you can terminate a program using the `exit()` function from the standard library. This function causes the program to terminate immediately, and it can optionally take an integer argument that represents the exit status. Here's a simple example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>  // Include the header for exit()

int main() {
    printf("Program is starting...\n");

    // Some code here...

    // Terminate the program
    exit(1);  // Exit with status 1, indicating an error or abnormal termination

    // More code here...
    return 0;  // This line will never be reached
}
```

In this example, `exit(1)` is used
